@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit and rollout
+
+## Audit findings
+
+- The original document had one inline stylesheet and one style attribute, with no CSP or companion security headers. Styles now live in the explicitly served `/styles.css`; no inline exceptions are required.
+- There are no client-side scripts, event handlers, HTML injection sinks, eval calls, window messaging, forms, browser storage, or application cookies. Script execution is denied entirely. Trusted Types is enforced with no policy creation allowed in supporting browsers; CSP still blocks scripts in browsers without Trusted Types support.
+- The only third-party dependency is the existing HTTPS Unsplash background image. Only that image origin is allowed. There are no external scripts or stylesheets requiring SRI.
+- There are no popup flows, embeds, webhooks, cross-site APIs, or browser hardware features. Framing is denied, unused permissions are disabled, and non-navigation cross-site requests are rejected using Fetch Metadata. Missing metadata is allowed for older clients; same-site subdomains are not automatically trusted.
+- The existing routes still render the same marketing page. Logout is a placeholder, with no server session to invalidate. It now sends no-store and Clear-Site-Data, which supporting browsers honor in secure contexts. Real authentication will require server-side session invalidation and a CSRF-protected POST logout.
+- No authentication or authorization system was introduced. These headers are browser defenses, not access control.
+
+## Current policy
+
+Enforcement is the default. The audited static page needs only same-origin styles and Unsplash images, so no nonce, script hash, script allowlist, unsafe-inline, or unsafe-eval exception is necessary. Plugins, base URL overrides, cross-origin form submission, frames, outbound script connections, and script execution are denied.
+
+COOP uses `same-origin-allow-popups`; CORP uses `same-origin`. COEP and document isolation are intentionally omitted: there is no shared-memory requirement, and enforcing them could unnecessarily break external images. No permissive CORS grant is added.
+
+All handled responses, including errors and logout, receive companion headers. The stylesheet has a CSS MIME type with nosniff. A referrer meta tag also protects the HTML if it is served without the Node handler, but full security policies require the response headers.
+
+## Deploy safely
+
+1. Terminate TLS at the deployment proxy, redirect HTTP to HTTPS there, and set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-actual-domain.example` (no trailing slash) in the Node process. This enables an absolute HTTPS Reporting-Endpoints destination and initial HSTS `max-age=300`. The app never trusts Host or forwarded headers to construct these settings. Local HTTP remains usable when PUBLIC_ORIGIN is unset.
+2. Forward the security response headers and `POST /security-reports` through the proxy. If serving files directly from a CDN, reproduce the policies there; copying HTML alone does not deploy them. Do not log report request bodies at the proxy or in tracing systems. Apply edge request limits and timeouts as well.
+3. For an existing deployment with untracked integrations, set `CSP_MODE=report-only` first. Basic object/base/framing restrictions and companion policies remain enforced while the full CSP is reported. Collect representative traffic for days to weeks, then remove that setting or set `CSP_MODE=enforce`. An invalid mode fails startup instead of silently disabling protection.
+4. Reports use the named Reporting API endpoint on configured HTTPS deployments, with `report-uri` as the compatibility fallback. Without PUBLIC_ORIGIN only the same-origin fallback is emitted. Reporting is best effort, not proof that a policy is compatible. See the [Reporting-Endpoints reference](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Reporting-Endpoints).
+5. Review aggregate `csp-violation` directive counts in application logs. Reports are untrusted and can be forged by anyone. The collector caps each body at 16 KiB, processes at most 20 batched reports, and accepts at most 60 report requests per minute per process. It retains no raw payload, URL, query string, script sample, IP, or user agent. This minimizes sensitive data retention at the cost of detailed diagnostics; distinguish extension noise from regressions using controlled traffic and source review.
+6. If a policy blocks a newly required integration, use report-only as a temporary rollback and audit the exact dependency before changing enforcement. Authorize future scripts with explicit template nonces or immutable hashes; do not broadly allow a script CDN or add unsafe-inline.
+7. After confirming HTTPS operation, increase HSTS at the edge or in security.js gradually. Audit every subdomain before adding includeSubDomains; do not preload during the initial rollout.
+
+## Verification performed
+
+`npm test` invokes the request handler with in-memory streams and mock responses. It covers markup/CSP compatibility, CSS delivery, rollout configuration, HTTPS origin validation, Fetch Metadata, method restrictions, HEAD, logout headers, both report formats, redaction, malformed reports, body limits, and rate limiting.
+
+`npm run build` checks JavaScript syntax without executing the server entrypoint. No browser verification, listening server, or deployed-header inspection was performed.
